{%- comment -%}
  Product gallery — port of components/product/product-gallery.tsx.

  A column of thumbnails beside the main crop. Thumbnails are buttons with
  aria-pressed, not links, because choosing an angle is not a navigation; the main
  image gets the selected shot rather than the page scrolling to it.

  Where a piece has only one shot the remaining slots are the labelled placeholder
  at the right ratio, so the rail is already its final size and shape. A gallery
  with one entry hides the rail altogether.

  The rail is a column beside the photograph from `lg` up and a scrolling row
  beneath it below that: 92px of thumbnails alongside leaves the main crop about
  260px on a phone, and the main crop is the thing being bought.

  Without JavaScript the first angle shows and the thumbnails are inert — the same
  as the React version, which was a client component for exactly this state.

  The main crop is 4 / 5, stated on the render call rather than left to
  media.liquid's square source default, so the real photograph and the empty
  state below draw the SAME proportion — which is the one thing placeholder mode
  exists to guarantee. Trousers are judged on length, so the portrait crop is the
  right one; it does take 10% off each side of a square source, so the eleven
  shots want eyeballing for clipped hems once they are imported.

  `priority` is the FIRST shot only, not every shot. It used to be `true` on all
  of them, which did nothing while media.liquid was dropping the parameter on the
  floor; now that the parameter is read, `true` on all of them would fetch an
  eight-angle gallery eagerly at fetchpriority="high" and starve the one image
  the page is actually judged on. The rest are lazy, and the hidden ones do not
  fetch until the thumbnails reveal them.

  The first main shot carries `view-transition-name: shot-<handle>`, the other
  half of the pair set on the catalogue card, so a card grows into the product
  page instead of the page swapping. Exactly one element per document may carry a
  given name, hence `forloop.first` AND the handle guard: in placeholder mode
  there is no product and no name is emitted at all.

  Accepts: product
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign clips = product.media | where: 'media_type', 'video'
  assign shots = product.images
  assign shot_count = clips.size | plus: shots.size

  comment
    Which slot opens, and why the film leads.

    A colourway chosen in the URL wins outright: `selected_variant` is nil
    unless `?variant=` is present, so this fires only when the reader actually
    picked one, and showing them anything but that colourway would be ignoring
    the choice they just made. Its photograph is found by id rather than by
    position, because the media order is the studio's and nothing guarantees
    the nth image belongs to the nth colourway.

    Otherwise the film opens if there is one. That is the instruction —
    the catalogue card is the main photograph, and opening the product plays
    the film. Where there is no film this falls through to image 0, which is
    that same main photograph, so the no-video product is unchanged.
  endcomment
  assign open_index = 0
  assign chosen = product.selected_variant.featured_image
  if chosen != blank
    for shot in shots
      if shot.id == chosen.id
        assign open_index = clips.size | plus: forloop.index0
        break
      endif
    endfor
  endif
-%}

<div class="flex flex-col-reverse gap-4 lg:flex-row" data-gallery>
  {%- if shot_count > 1 -%}
    <ul
      aria-label="{{ 'product.gallery' | t }}"
      class="wl-rail flex gap-3 overflow-x-auto lg:w-23 lg:shrink-0 lg:flex-col lg:gap-4 lg:overflow-visible"
    >
      {%- comment -%}
        The film's thumbnail is its own poster frame, so the rail reads as one
        strip rather than a video control bolted onto a row of photographs.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- for clip in clips -%}
        <li class="w-20 shrink-0 lg:w-full">
          <button
            type="button"
            data-gallery-thumb="{{ forloop.index0 }}"
            aria-pressed="{% if forloop.index0 == open_index %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}"
            class="wl-press-soft block w-full cursor-pointer overflow-hidden rounded-xs border transition-colors duration-(--duration-base) aria-pressed:border-brand border-border hover:border-line"
          >
            {% render 'media', image: clip.preview_image, sizes: '(min-width: 1024px) 92px, 80px', ratio: '4 / 5', label: 'Angle', class: 'rounded-none' %}
            <span class="sr-only">{{ 'product.show_video' | t }}</span>
          </button>
        </li>
      {%- endfor -%}
      {%- for shot in shots -%}
        {%- assign slot = clips.size | plus: forloop.index0 -%}
        <li class="w-20 shrink-0 lg:w-full">
          <button
            type="button"
            data-gallery-thumb="{{ slot }}"
            aria-pressed="{% if slot == open_index %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}"
            class="wl-press-soft block w-full cursor-pointer overflow-hidden rounded-xs border transition-colors duration-(--duration-base) aria-pressed:border-brand border-border hover:border-line"
          >
            {% render 'media', image: shot, sizes: '(min-width: 1024px) 92px, 80px', ratio: '4 / 5', label: 'Angle', class: 'rounded-none' %}
            <span class="sr-only">{{ 'product.show_image' | t }} {{ forloop.index }}</span>
          </button>
        </li>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </ul>
  {%- endif -%}

  <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
    {%- comment -%}
      The film is muted, looping and inline because it autoplays: a browser
      only autoplays a muted video, and `playsinline` is what stops iOS taking
      it fullscreen the moment it starts. Controls stay on — this is a garment
      being shown, not a hero band, so a reader who wants to stop it should be
      able to. theme.js pauses it by reaching for the slot's own `video`
      element when the reader switches to a photograph; an off-screen video
      that keeps decoding is frame budget spent on something nobody is
      looking at.

      `media_tag` is given only the parameters it documents. Sizing is a CSS
      rule on `[data-gallery] video` in theme-src/theme.css rather than a
      `class:` argument, because an argument the filter does not know is
      dropped silently and the video would then size itself.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- for clip in clips -%}
      <div data-gallery-main="{{ forloop.index0 }}" {% unless forloop.index0 == open_index %}hidden{% endunless %}>
        <div style="aspect-ratio:4 / 5" class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-tone">
          {{ clip | media_tag: image_size: '1200x', autoplay: true, loop: true, muted: true, controls: true }}
        </div>
      </div>
    {%- endfor -%}

    {%- for shot in shots -%}
      {%- assign slot = clips.size | plus: forloop.index0 -%}
      <div
        data-gallery-main="{{ slot }}"
        {% unless slot == open_index %}hidden{% endunless %}
        {% if forloop.first and product.handle != blank %}style="view-transition-name: shot-{{ product.handle }}"{% endif %}
      >
        {% render 'media', image: shot, sizes: '(min-width: 1024px) 45vw, 100vw', ratio: '4 / 5', priority: forloop.first, label: 'Product photo' %}
      </div>
    {%- endfor -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      The placeholder carries the view-transition name too, so the morph pairs
      with the catalogue card before the photography exists. snippets/product-card
      names its crop whenever `product` is set, not only when there is an image —
      so without this the two guards disagree and a piece with no shot yet emits a
      name on one page and none on the other, which is a morph that silently never
      runs. Naming both keeps the effect reviewable on an empty store, which is
      the whole point of placeholder mode.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if shot_count == 0 -%}
      <div{% if product.handle != blank %} style="view-transition-name: shot-{{ product.handle }}"{% endif %}>
        {% render 'media', image: nil, sizes: '(min-width: 1024px) 45vw, 100vw', ratio: '4 / 5', label: 'Product photo' %}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</div>
